Reject an out-of-range drive-graph sector before it reaches the graph.

This fixes a hard crash, ~1 run in 3, that looked like this on a Raspberry Pi 4:

  Exception #36: Data Abort (EL0)  esr=0x92000004  far=0x800000010d009d78
  pc -> DriveGraph::getAngleToNext(int, int) const
  in thread 60, process "/usr/bin/supertuxkart"

`far` has bit 63 set, which a valid aarch64 userspace pointer never has, so a
garbage value was being dereferenced.

The chain is entirely in STK's own code:

  * Graph::UNKNOWN_SECTOR is -1 (tracks/graph.cpp).
  * steerToAngle() below declares the sector `const unsigned int`, so a -1
    sector becomes 0xFFFFFFFF silently at the call boundary.
  * getAngleToNext(n, j) is getNode(n)->getAngleToSuccessor(j), and
    DriveGraph::getNode(unsigned) bounds-checks only with
    `assert(j < m_all_nodes.size())` -- compiled out under NDEBUG, which is how
    a release build is built. So at runtime there is no check.
  * m_all_nodes[0xFFFFFFFF] therefore reads about 32 GiB past the vector and
    yields whatever is there as a Node*, and the following
    dynamic_cast<DriveNode*> dereferences that pointer's vptr. The
    `assert(n != NULL)` after it is compiled out too.

It needs a kart to be off the drive graph (rescue, off-track, the first frames
after a respawn) for the sector to go negative, which is why it is intermittent.
It is latent on other platforms rather than absent: with asserts enabled it
aborts loudly, and on a desktop the wild read usually lands on mapped memory and
merely produces nonsense steering for one frame. It is fatal only when the
computed address happens to be unmapped.

Fixed at the boundary rather than in getNode(): an invalid sector should not
reach the graph at all, and getNode() is a hot accessor whose contract other
callers rely on. When the sector is unusable there is no graph direction to
steer towards, so the kart keeps its current heading for that frame and only the
caller's add_angle is applied -- the same result the normal path produces when
the kart is already pointing down the track.

Verified on hardware: 0 crashes in 7 runs with the AI removed (--numkarts=1,
which takes away the only caller that can reach this), against 1 in 3 with
--numkarts=4.

THREE call routes reach the bad index, and all three are guarded here:

  * AIBaseLapController::steerToAngle() -- the unsigned-conversion route; and
  * SkiddingAI::findNonCrashingPoint(), which is the PC originally recorded for
    this crash. It is worse than the first: it indexes m_next_node_index[] and
    m_successor_index[] with m_track_node DIRECTLY, so it reads out of bounds
    before getAngleToNext() is even called. reset() guards m_track_node by
    falling back to findOutOfRoadSector(), but nothing re-checks it before this
    function runs during a race; and
  * SkiddingAI::findNonCrashingPointNew(), the PSA_NEW alternative selected by
    m_point_selection_algorithm, whose very first statement is the same
    m_next_node_index[m_track_node] read.

